 ## Private Cache
 
-Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Before admitting a completed sheet, evict enough existing sheets to remain within that bound. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
+Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics. Entries with pending render jobs are not cache entries and are never eviction candidates; a failed render admits nothing to the cache.
 
-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+The cache uses a 2Q replacement policy organized over preview keys.
+
+### Queue structure
+
+- **A1in** — freshly rendered sheets with at most one reference so far, in insertion order. Bounded at 3 GiB (about 1,536 sheets at 2 MiB per sheet).
+- **Am** — sheets that have been referenced at least twice, in least-recently-used order. Bounded at 9 GiB (about 4,608 sheets).
+- **A1out** — keys of sheets evicted from A1in before any reuse. Holds keys only, no JPEG data. Bounded at 3,000 keys.
+
+The A1in and Am bounds sum to the 12 GiB budget, so the global limit holds by construction; each queue evicts within its own bound before admitting.
+
+### Admission and promotion
+
+- When a render completes for a key listed in A1out, admit the sheet to Am's most-recently-used position and remove the key from A1out. The sheet was already evicted once and requested again, so it has demonstrated reuse.
+- When a render completes for any other key, admit the sheet to A1in.
+- When a request hits a cached sheet, promote it to Am's most-recently-used position, removing it from A1in if it was there; admitting by promotion evicts from Am first if Am is at its bound. A single reference never enters Am, so one-pass screening traffic cannot displace reused sheets.
+
+### Eviction
+
+- When admitting a completed sheet to A1in would exceed the A1in bound, first evict the oldest A1in sheet: free its JPEG data and record its key at the most-recently-used end of A1out. When A1out exceeds its key bound, drop its oldest key.
+- When admitting a sheet to Am would exceed the Am bound, first evict the least-recently-used Am sheet and drop its key; Am evictions do not enter A1out.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Cache replacement policy | 2Q (A1in / A1out / Am) | LRU; frequency-based eviction with aging | The workload alternates one-pass screenings with repeated comparison of a small set. 2Q keeps screening scan traffic out of the reused queue so comparison sheets survive long screenings, at bookkeeping cost that stays private to the preview worker. LRU adapts quickly but a long screening evicts the comparison set; aged frequency counts retain repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and keep stale favorites after a production switch. |
+| Promotion rule | A cached sheet enters Am on its second reference (a hit in A1in, or a render completing for a key in A1out) | Classic 2Q, where only A1out ghost hits promote and A1in hits do not | A comparison sheet's early references all fall inside A1in's window, so ghost-only promotion would leave it unprotected during exactly the screenings this policy must survive. A scan touches each sheet once, so second-reference promotion still excludes one-pass traffic. |
+| Queue bounds | A1in 3 GiB, Am 9 GiB, A1out 3,000 keys | Even split; dynamic split; larger A1out | Keeps a screening's one-pass traffic inside A1in while leaving three quarters of the budget for reused sheets; keys-only A1out makes the ghost bound cheap. Initial values, to be tuned against pilot hit rates. |
+
